     "result_text_tail": "ied to us. It ends with the decisions for the board, next steps and the conditions for reopening the question. Two appendix slides give the full per-order numbers and the judgement calls.\n\n**Choices I made in place of asking you (also listed in Appendix B):**\n- **What \"contribution\" means:** the pack doesn't define it, so I show it both before and after store fixed costs. The plan is positive both ways, so the conclusion doesn't depend on the choice.\n- **The $9.5M:** I treated it as all company spending to month 18, which is the reading most favourable to the plan. If it covers Parcelbird Now only, cash runs out (\u2212$4.7M).\n- **Runway:** cash divided by today's $0.4M monthly burn, held flat. One thing the board should see: even with no new spending, flat burn reaches the 12-month minimum in about 18 months. The deck says the core business needs to cut its burn whatever the board decides.\n- **Inferences, not pack facts, and labelled as such:** the risk of competing with grocer customers, and the pop-up's growth path (about 180 to 410 orders a day).\n- **Format:** an editable PowerPoint file. The sandbox blocked headless Chrome, so I checked layout with my own renderer instead of opening the file in PowerPoint. All 16 slides were checked by eye and pass an automatic text-overflow check, but spacing in PowerPoint may differ slightly.\n\nFiles are in `./deliverable/`:\n- `Parcelbird_Now_board_deck.pptx`\n- `Parcelbird_Now_board_deck_preview.pdf`\n- `source/` (scripts to rebuild the deck)"
